  "final_message": "I haven't removed `channel`. Two things still use it, so the premise doesn't hold.\n\n- **analytics-ingest requires it.** `analytics-ingest/src/core/events.ts:34` reads `channel` from `OrderPlaced` with `oneOf(\"channel\", CHANNELS)`. Per its README and `test/ingest.test.ts:158`, a missing `channel` makes ingestion reject the whole event with a `ContractViolationError`. `src/ingest.ts:17` writes it to the `orders.channel` column. If we dropped it from the event, every order placed would be rejected by analytics.\n- **orders-service reads it.** `src/api/legacyExport.ts:6` and `:18` export `order.channel` as a CSV column, and `test/adapters.test.ts:137` asserts that header.\n\nbilling-service doesn't need it. Its test only builds an event with `channel: undefined`.\n\nThe `channel` field also goes beyond the event and model. It is validated in `core/order.ts` and required in the `POST /orders` request via `requestParsing.ts`. The `createApp` example in CLAUDE.md and the README also include it, so removing it would change the public API.\n\nSome options:\n1. Leave it as is.\n2. Have analytics-ingest make `channel` optional first. That's another team's repo, and I have it read-only. Once that ships, I can remove it from orders-service.\n3. Remove it only from the `OrderPlaced` event and keep it on the order model, which would still break analytics-ingest.\n\nWhich do you want? If you go with option 2, tell me whether to also drop the legacy export column and the `POST /orders` input field.",
